and here in new york opening statements  begin this morning in r kelly's federal  trial the r b singer is charged with  racketeering for the alleged sexual  exploitation of children and a series of  other counts related to illegal sexual  activity r kelly denies all those  charges attorney steve greenberg has  represented r kelly since 2018  he left the defense team here in new  york last month over disagreements with  two other lawyers on the team but he  still represents kelly in illinois and  minnesota and he joins us only on cbs  this morning mr greenberg good morning  i guess we should begin with your  expectations for the opening remarks at  trial today with the bigger  understanding that this is a legal team  you're no longer part of  right well i i think what they have to  do in opening statements today is  uh just convince the jurors that r kelly  is not the monster that the  public has come to think  that he is the government i expect is  going to have a very  detailed opening they're going to  present all sorts of uh stories that  these women they suppose are going to  tell they they allege are going to tell  but then what the defense has to do is  they have to humanize r kelly they have  to tell the jurors how this is someone  who started out singing in subways and  really as a celebrity  he just sort of lived his life and made  his music what people are going to find  out is that  the  sort of the moral view that we all have  and and kind of the me too movement has  changed the way we looked at things but  in the moment things were happening we  didn't look at them that way and they  weren't illegal so is the argument going  to be that these women are lying or that  what the women are alleging may be  disagreeable  and true but it's not a crime  well i think it's going to be a  combination of all of that  i think that some of the women are just  out and out lying i think that some of  the women are exaggerating i think that  some of the women are perhaps telling  the truth about what happened but it's  just not a crime what the government has  done here is they they've used the rico  statute which really is intended to  prosecute gangs and mobsters and they're  trying to prosecute an individual for a  bunch of acts where the statute of  limitations has expired or they were  minor acts and they've sort of blown  them out of proportion by using this  rico law in my opinion it's it's just a  complete uh perversion of the intent of  the rico law so when you talk about  sweeping up these minor acts into the  bigger crime under the rico law what  what are you considering to be a minor  act what would the defense be willing to  concede as true to try to get r kelly  off in this case  well i think the one that everyone's  heard about the most is an allegation  that he gave people herpes which  actually is not what the law says  uh the new york prosecutors are using a  new york state law and that's the only  reason the case is actually in new york  that's how they get what we call venue  and they're using that misdemeanor law  that no one's ever been prosecuted  against  to try and  get a foothold in new york on a felony  case  when a prosecutor has to get  truly creative as they have in this case  then that's probably not what the law  was intended to punish or prohibit  what do you say steve to people that say  listen there have been rumors for years  about r kelly abusing women and and  certainly young girls  what do you say when you say the defense  has to humanize him or even show that r  kelly is a victim how can they do that  when so many of these women and young  girls are telling the same stories who  have no ties to each other  they can't all be lying can they  well and everyone in a criminal case  people don't all have to be lying for a  defendant to prevail in a criminal case  um  what's abuse gail that's really what  this comes down to so you've got  different categories  of offenses here you've got  alleged minor victims  some of which i think you're going to  find out lied about their age their  families lied about their age and they  really tried to capitalize on r kelly  and extort him for money  and then you've got other things which  they've alleged like he wanted people to  call him daddy now how is that any  different from me saying i want to be  called honey or sweetheart  maybe it's a different word but it's  still the same concept so steve's  teaching  the difference between being called  daddy and being called sweetheart and  honey in the context that these girls  were saying but that they were kept  locked up these are these are grown gale  these are grown no no these are not  capital active rooms these are grown  women uh for the most part who who made  a choice to be with an individual who  was a celebrity when they talk about  being in a locked room  they would go to the studio they would  be told wait in this room someone will  come and get you there's no chains  there's no hooks in the floor like we  supposedly heard about in that so-called  documentary there's none of that if i go  to visit somebody at a police station i  get put in a room and told i can't leave  the room if you go to certain places you  get told wait in the room these women  waited sometimes three four five hours  while he was recording music but that's  not being locked in a room forever and  held days on end like you see in some of  these uh sex slave kind of cases  yeah and that's right and that's why we  have trials yeah that's why we have  trials that's why we have  cross-examination and that's why we  should all keep an open mind until the  end so you're and you're saying mental  abuse if that's what it is isn't a crime  well mental abuse isn't a crime that's  absolutely correct so what's happened  here is we've got the me too movement  and so we look at something and we say  well this was abuse but is it against  the law that's really what you have to  do and that's what they have to do an  opening statement today is get that  across to the jurors that there's a  difference between what's morally  incorrect or me too incorrect and what's  legally incorrect steve you were there  that day we did the interview with r  kelly and i'm curious about your um  characterization of that and the reason  why i'm asking you specifically is  because later r kelly was said to me or  sent word to me that he was thanking me  for giving him the platform to express  himself because we got to see he says  his passion and his pain do you think  that interview hurt or helped him  i think to some people it helped and to  some people it hurt i you know if our  kelly had sat there with you perfectly  stoic everyone would have said he  doesn't feel bad about anything he's got  no emotion he's got no feeling he got  very upset during that interview that  was very real that was how he felt that  is how i feel that he's being taken  advantage of here the problem is no  matter what the man does people are  going to criticize but all that matters  gail and you know this is what those 12  people in the courtroom think in some  three or four weeks from now all right  all right steve greenberg thank you very  much we appreciate you getting up with  us

